-- =============================================================================
-- Where things stand:
--   SELECT * FROM DAAS_UAT.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI_3641_RUNS ORDER BY started_at DESC;
--   SELECT * FROM DAAS_UAT.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI_3641_CHECKS ORDER BY checked_at DESC, check_no;
--
-- A call failed with an error, or a check failed: nothing was kept (the run
-- rolled back) and the run's row in DAASI_3641_RUNS says why. Fix the cause and
-- call again.
--
-- A call was cut off (cancelled, or stopped by a statement timeout; closing a
-- tab or losing the connection does NOT stop a call): it still holds the run
-- lock and its transaction, and every procedure refuses to run in that
-- worksheet until it is cleaned up. Once Query History shows the CALL is no
-- longer running, in a NEW worksheet, as the same user:
--   CALL DAAS_UAT.DAAS_TEMP_WORK.DAASI_3641_RECOVER('<run_id from DAASI_3641_RUNS>');
-- then call RUN_ALL again (in the new worksheet).
--
-- Finding the statement behind "... on line N at position P": N counts from the
-- line holding the opening $$ of that procedure's file (that line is 1). In
-- Snowsight's "SQL definition" view of the procedure, the same statement is at
-- line N + 5 (the view adds five header lines).
--
-- "SQL execution internal error ... incident ...": Snowflake itself crashed on
-- that statement; it is not a SQL mistake. (Monday's crash, 300002:3259654597,
-- was INSERT ... VALUES with bound values; none of the procedures uses that form
-- now, and PREFLIGHT row 16 proves the forms they do use.) Send Claude the
-- incident number and the statement's line.
